1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,608
December fourth, eighteen seventy two.

2
00:00:02,828 --> 00:00:08,185
Somewhere between the Azores and Portugal, a ship is sailing along with nobody steering it.

3
00:00:08,685 --> 00:00:09,615
No captain.

4
00:00:09,835 --> 00:00:10,633
No crew.

5
00:00:10,853 --> 00:00:12,027
No passengers.

6
00:00:12,247 --> 00:00:16,336
Just a ship, minding its own business, in the middle of the Atlantic.

7
00:00:17,136 --> 00:00:20,405
Which, for a ship, is a very bold lifestyle choice.

8
00:00:21,805 --> 00:00:22,334
Hi.

9
00:00:22,554 --> 00:00:23,439
I'm Archie.

10
00:00:23,659 --> 00:00:31,191
Welcome to The Quiet Archive, where I read the boring paperwork behind famous mysteries, so you get the good parts.

11
00:00:31,691 --> 00:00:33,119
Today, the Mary Celeste.

12
00:00:33,339 --> 00:00:35,363
The most famous ghost ship in history.

13
00:00:35,863 --> 00:00:37,023
And here's the twist.

14
00:00:37,243 --> 00:00:39,541
Half of what you've heard about it is made up.

15
00:00:40,041 --> 00:00:41,833
Warm cups of tea on the table?

16
00:00:42,053 --> 00:00:42,729
Made up.

17
00:00:42,949 --> 00:00:44,287
Half eaten breakfast?

18
00:00:44,507 --> 00:00:45,183
Made up.

19
00:00:45,403 --> 00:00:47,416
A ship's cat staring into the void?

20
00:00:47,636 --> 00:00:50,785
Absolutely made up, and frankly a little dramatic.

21
00:00:51,285 --> 00:00:52,731
So let's do this properly.

22
00:00:52,951 --> 00:00:57,784
What does the record actually say, and what really happened to the ten people on board?

23
00:00:59,184 --> 00:01:01,094
November, eighteen seventy two.

24
00:01:01,314 --> 00:01:02,397
New York harbor.

25
00:01:02,617 --> 00:01:06,491
The Mary Celeste is loading up for a trip to Genoa, in Italy.

26
00:01:06,991 --> 00:01:08,992
Her captain is Benjamin Briggs.

27
00:01:09,212 --> 00:01:17,357
Thirty seven years old, experienced, sensible, a man who by all accounts did not drink and did not do anything silly.

28
00:01:17,857 --> 00:01:22,383
Coming along with him are his wife, Sarah, and their two year old daughter, Sophia.

29
00:01:22,603 --> 00:01:25,752
Their older son stays home in Massachusetts for school.

30
00:01:26,252 --> 00:01:27,343
Plus seven crew.

31
00:01:27,563 --> 00:01:28,828
Ten people in total.

32
00:01:29,328 --> 00:01:30,277
And the cargo?

33
00:01:30,497 --> 00:01:34,224
One thousand seven hundred and one barrels of alcohol.

34
00:01:35,024 --> 00:01:36,246
Not the fun kind.

35
00:01:36,466 --> 00:01:37,922
Industrial alcohol.

36
00:01:38,142 --> 00:01:40,683
Basically a floating barrel of paint thinner.

37
00:01:41,183 --> 00:01:44,007
On November seventh, they sail out of New York.

38
00:01:44,227 --> 00:01:47,048
And that is the last time anyone sees any of them.

39
00:01:48,448 --> 00:01:50,155
Fast forward almost a month.

40
00:01:50,375 --> 00:01:54,415
Another ship, the Dei Gratia, is crossing the same stretch of ocean.

41
00:01:54,915 --> 00:01:58,019
Her lookout spots a ship ahead, moving strangely.

42
00:01:58,239 --> 00:01:59,250
Sails half set.

43
00:01:59,470 --> 00:02:00,785
Zig zagging a little.

44
00:02:01,005 --> 00:02:02,504
Not answering signals.

45
00:02:03,004 --> 00:02:09,427
The Dei Gratia's captain, David Morehouse, takes a look through his telescope and sees something very weird.

46
00:02:09,927 --> 00:02:11,269
Nobody at the wheel.

47
00:02:11,489 --> 00:02:12,675
Nobody on deck.

48
00:02:12,895 --> 00:02:14,122
Nobody anywhere.

49
00:02:15,522 --> 00:02:20,927
So he sends a few men over in a small boat, led by his first mate, Oliver Deveau.

50
00:02:21,427 --> 00:02:26,348
Deveau climbs aboard and finds a ship that is messy, but very much not sinking.

51
00:02:26,848 --> 00:02:28,207
Some sails are torn.

52
00:02:28,427 --> 00:02:29,905
Some rigging is damaged.

53
00:02:30,125 --> 00:02:31,451
Two hatches are open.

54
00:02:31,951 --> 00:02:34,665
There's about three and a half feet of water in the hold.

55
00:02:34,885 --> 00:02:40,400
Which sounds scary, until you learn that for a ship that size, it's annoying, not deadly.

56
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:44,969
The ship's compass housing is knocked out of place, with the glass broken.

57
00:02:45,189 --> 00:02:51,127
There's a makeshift sounding rod, a stick for measuring water in the hold, just lying on the deck.

58
00:02:51,627 --> 00:02:52,616
And the lifeboat?

59
00:02:52,836 --> 00:02:53,409
Gone.

60
00:02:54,809 --> 00:02:57,502
Down in the captain's cabin, it gets stranger.

61
00:02:58,002 --> 00:03:00,228
The personal belongings are all still there.

62
00:03:00,448 --> 00:03:01,185
Clothes.

63
00:03:01,405 --> 00:03:02,606
The family's things.

64
00:03:02,826 --> 00:03:05,565
Even a sword, still in its sheath, under the bed.

65
00:03:06,065 --> 00:03:08,195
There's plenty of food and fresh water.

66
00:03:08,415 --> 00:03:09,558
Six months' worth.

67
00:03:10,058 --> 00:03:12,271
But some very specific things are missing.

68
00:03:12,491 --> 00:03:14,637
The captain's navigation instruments.

69
00:03:14,857 --> 00:03:16,371
Most of the ship's papers.

70
00:03:16,871 --> 00:03:21,752
And the logbook is still there, with its last entry dated November twenty fifth.

71
00:03:22,552 --> 00:03:24,683
Nine days before the ship was found.

72
00:03:26,083 --> 00:03:27,884
Here's where that gets interesting.

73
00:03:28,104 --> 00:03:32,585
That last entry puts the ship near Santa Maria island, in the Azores.

74
00:03:33,085 --> 00:03:40,712
She was found about four hundred nautical miles away, still heading roughly the right way, on her own, for over a week.

75
00:03:41,212 --> 00:03:46,454
Which means a ship with nobody on board sailed itself across a big chunk of ocean.

76
00:03:47,254 --> 00:03:50,850
Honestly, better navigation than I manage in a parking lot.

77
00:03:52,250 --> 00:03:52,779
Now.

78
00:03:52,999 --> 00:03:56,865
Stop here for a second, because this is where the legend kicks in.

79
00:03:57,365 --> 00:03:59,049
There were no half eaten meals.

80
00:03:59,269 --> 00:04:00,648
There was no warm tea.

81
00:04:00,868 --> 00:04:02,827
Nobody found a still smoking pipe.

82
00:04:03,327 --> 00:04:08,670
Deveau described the ship as wet and messy, not a perfectly frozen moment in time.

83
00:04:09,170 --> 00:04:11,353
So where did all that creepy stuff come from?

84
00:04:11,573 --> 00:04:12,534
We'll get to that.

85
00:04:12,754 --> 00:04:14,969
Spoiler, it involves Sherlock Holmes.

86
00:04:16,369 --> 00:04:22,063
The Dei Gratia's crew sailed the Mary Celeste to Gibraltar and asked for a salvage reward.

87
00:04:22,283 --> 00:04:23,327
Totally normal.

88
00:04:23,547 --> 00:04:27,101
Finding an abandoned ship was basically a lottery ticket.

89
00:04:27,601 --> 00:04:32,557
But the attorney general there, Frederick Solly-Flood, was not in a normal mood.

90
00:04:33,057 --> 00:04:35,718
He was convinced something terrible had happened.

91
00:04:35,938 --> 00:04:40,280
Maybe the crew got drunk on the cargo, murdered the family, and escaped.

92
00:04:40,780 --> 00:04:45,280
He pointed to marks on the ship's bow, and stains on a sword, as evidence.

93
00:04:45,780 --> 00:04:47,256
Then the tests came back.

94
00:04:47,476 --> 00:04:49,020
The stains were not blood.

95
00:04:49,240 --> 00:04:53,081
And a naval inspector said the marks on the bow were natural wear.

96
00:04:53,581 --> 00:05:01,192
Also, the crew could not have gotten drunk on that cargo, unless they were very determined to have the worst night of their lives.

97
00:05:01,692 --> 00:05:05,184
In the end, the salvors got paid, but less than they hoped.

98
00:05:05,404 --> 00:05:08,435
About one fifth of the value of the ship and cargo.

99
00:05:09,835 --> 00:05:10,846
So, the legend.

100
00:05:11,066 --> 00:05:16,844
Eleven years later, a young doctor who wanted to be a writer published a short story about the ship.

101
00:05:17,344 --> 00:05:19,361
His name was Arthur Conan Doyle.

102
00:05:19,581 --> 00:05:20,166
Yes.

103
00:05:20,386 --> 00:05:21,701
Sherlock Holmes guy.

104
00:05:21,921 --> 00:05:23,304
Before Sherlock Holmes.

105
00:05:23,804 --> 00:05:33,705
He renamed the ship the Marie Celeste, changed the captain's name, changed the route, invented passengers, and added a villain who leads a bloody mutiny.

106
00:05:34,205 --> 00:05:35,161
It was fiction.

107
00:05:35,381 --> 00:05:37,182
He never pretended otherwise.

108
00:05:37,402 --> 00:05:41,134
But it was so good that people started mixing it up with the real thing.

109
00:05:41,634 --> 00:05:48,841
And that's why, to this day, a lot of people call her the Marie Celeste, and picture a cup of tea that never existed.

110
00:05:50,241 --> 00:05:51,600
So what really happened?

111
00:05:51,820 --> 00:05:53,189
Nobody knows for sure.

112
00:05:53,409 --> 00:05:57,199
But the theories range from reasonable to, let's say, creative.

113
00:05:57,699 --> 00:05:58,581
Theory one.

114
00:05:58,801 --> 00:05:59,484
Pirates.

115
00:05:59,984 --> 00:06:02,118
Except pirates usually take things.

116
00:06:02,338 --> 00:06:06,988
And everything valuable was still on board, including the family's belongings.

117
00:06:07,488 --> 00:06:08,409
Theory two.

118
00:06:08,629 --> 00:06:09,605
A sea monster.

119
00:06:09,825 --> 00:06:11,656
Specifically, a giant squid.

120
00:06:12,456 --> 00:06:12,965
No.

121
00:06:13,465 --> 00:06:14,419
Theory three.

122
00:06:14,639 --> 00:06:15,789
Insurance fraud.

123
00:06:16,009 --> 00:06:18,975
Maybe Briggs and Morehouse cooked up a scheme together.

124
00:06:19,475 --> 00:06:27,786
Problem is, Briggs owned part of the ship himself, and walking away from it into a tiny lifeboat is a terrible way to get rich.

125
00:06:28,286 --> 00:06:29,233
Theory four.

126
00:06:29,453 --> 00:06:30,359
A waterspout.

127
00:06:30,579 --> 00:06:37,660
A sort of tornado over water, which could have messed with the pumps and the readings and made the ship look like it was sinking.

128
00:06:38,160 --> 00:06:39,110
Theory five.

129
00:06:39,330 --> 00:06:40,326
The alcohol.

130
00:06:41,726 --> 00:06:43,007
Remember the cargo.

131
00:06:43,227 --> 00:06:47,146
When the barrels were unloaded in Genoa, nine of them were empty.

132
00:06:47,646 --> 00:06:51,927
Some versions of this theory say alcohol fumes leaked into the hold.

133
00:06:52,147 --> 00:06:58,830
Maybe there was a rumble, a pop, a whiff of vapor, and Captain Briggs thought, this whole ship might explode.

134
00:06:59,330 --> 00:07:05,356
If you're the captain, with your wife and toddler on board, and you think you're sitting on a bomb, what do you do?

135
00:07:05,856 --> 00:07:07,717
You get everyone into the lifeboat.

136
00:07:07,937 --> 00:07:10,077
You tie it to the ship with a long rope.

137
00:07:10,297 --> 00:07:13,104
And you wait at a safe distance to see what happens.

138
00:07:13,604 --> 00:07:17,596
And if that rope snaps in rough weather, the ship sails away without you.

139
00:07:18,996 --> 00:07:22,840
Another version is even more ordinary, and maybe more likely.

140
00:07:23,340 --> 00:07:25,972
The ship had recently been used to carry coal.

141
00:07:26,192 --> 00:07:36,122
A documentary in two thousand seven suggested the pumps may have been clogged, while the ship's chronometer, the clock used for navigation, may have been off.

142
00:07:36,622 --> 00:07:46,506
Put that together, and you get a captain who thinks his ship is taking on water faster than it can be pumped, and who also thinks he's closer to land than he really is.

143
00:07:47,006 --> 00:07:48,751
So he does the sensible thing.

144
00:07:48,971 --> 00:07:53,297
Everyone into the boat, head for Santa Maria island, wait for help.

145
00:07:54,197 --> 00:07:55,693
Except the ship didn't sink.

146
00:07:55,913 --> 00:07:57,995
And the lifeboat was never seen again.

147
00:07:59,395 --> 00:08:00,601
And the ship herself?

148
00:08:00,821 --> 00:08:03,887
She had one of the most cursed careers in maritime history.

149
00:08:04,387 --> 00:08:19,633
After passing through a string of owners, in eighteen eighty five her captain, a man named Gilman Parker, loaded her with mostly worthless cargo, insured it for a lot of money, and deliberately ran her onto a reef near Haiti.

150
00:08:20,133 --> 00:08:21,081
He got caught.

151
00:08:21,301 --> 00:08:26,478
The case fell apart in a messy trial, but the Mary Celeste's sailing days were over.

152
00:08:26,978 --> 00:08:31,676
In two thousand one, an expedition said it had found her wreck on that reef.

153
00:08:31,896 --> 00:08:38,586
Then the wood was tested, and it came from trees that were still growing years after the Mary Celeste was lost.

154
00:08:39,086 --> 00:08:41,303
So, even her wreck might be a mystery.

155
00:08:42,703 --> 00:08:44,428
So here's the honest version.

156
00:08:44,928 --> 00:08:45,866
Documented.

157
00:08:46,086 --> 00:08:48,557
The ship was found sailing with no one aboard.

158
00:08:48,777 --> 00:08:50,139
The lifeboat was gone.

159
00:08:50,359 --> 00:08:53,177
Food, water and belongings were left behind.

160
00:08:53,397 --> 00:08:55,606
Instruments and most papers were missing.

161
00:08:55,826 --> 00:08:58,095
Nine barrels of alcohol were empty.

162
00:08:58,315 --> 00:09:02,101
The last log entry was off the Azores, nine days earlier.

163
00:09:02,601 --> 00:09:03,331
Legend.

164
00:09:03,551 --> 00:09:04,528
The warm tea.

165
00:09:04,748 --> 00:09:06,201
The half eaten breakfast.

166
00:09:06,421 --> 00:09:07,195
The mutiny.

167
00:09:07,415 --> 00:09:08,091
The cat.

168
00:09:08,311 --> 00:09:09,289
The name Marie.

169
00:09:09,789 --> 00:09:12,944
What probably happened is less spooky and more human.

170
00:09:13,164 --> 00:09:19,074
A careful captain made a fast decision to protect his family and crew, and the sea did the rest.

171
00:09:20,474 --> 00:09:21,818
But I want to hear from you.

172
00:09:22,038 --> 00:09:25,704
Fumes, pumps, waterspout, or something nobody has thought of yet?

173
00:09:26,204 --> 00:09:27,448
Tell me in the comments.

174
00:09:27,668 --> 00:09:28,601
I read them.

175
00:09:28,821 --> 00:09:31,525
It's literally my job, I'm an archivist.

176
00:09:32,025 --> 00:09:36,103
If you like true stories that history never quite finished, subscribe.

177
00:09:36,323 --> 00:09:40,706
I'm Archie, this is The Quiet Archive, and I'll see you in the next file.
